# Chapter 4, third part: Global, Format.  Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Format [format-format]
.ix Global Format
.ix Global Format panel
.ix Level 1 Format
.ix Level Format panels
.ix Global section settings
.ix Format panels, ring of
Global, Format sets the formats that a whole document takes unless a more
local setting overrides them ({n:format-order}).  It holds the settings of
the headlines and of the text, the spacing between blocks and sections, the
numbering of sections, and the same again for each of the five levels of
section.  The subjects that these settings are about (attributes, indents,
justification, section tags, tab stops, spacing, fonts) are each explained
after the panels, in the order in which the fields use them.

.h3 How to reach the command [format-format-reach]
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Format (the first entry; the hint line
reads "Global section settings"), as described in {x:format-global-menu}.  The
first panel is Global Format (headline and text).
.pic pictures/format-global-format.png 5 Global Format (headline and text).

.h3 The fourteen panels [format-format-panels]
.ix Global Format (headline and text)
.ix Global Format (spacing)
.ix Level Format (headline and text)
.ix Level Format (spacing)
.ix PgUp, PgDn in Global Format
The global settings and each of the five levels have two panels, so that
every panel fits on the screen: a panel of the rulers (attributes,
justification, indent, font and tab stops of the headline and of the text)
and a panel of the spacing and of the numbering.  The global settings have
a third panel, for the numbering of the printed lines
({n:format-fields-linenum}), and a fourth, for the contents and the lists
({n:format-fields-lists}).  That makes fourteen panels in a ring, in this
order: Global Format (headline and text), Global Format (spacing), Global
Format (line numbering), Global Format (contents and lists), Level 1 Format
(headline and text), Level 1 Format
(spacing), and so on to Level 5
Format (spacing).  {k:PgUp} goes forward in that order and {k:PgDn} backward;
after the last panel {k:PgUp} returns to the first.  {k:Ins} applies all
fourteen at once and {k:Esc} none.  A section of level 6 or
deeper takes the global settings.

.h3 Global Format (headline and text) [format-fields-rulers]
.ix Headline Attributes
.ix Headline Justification
.ix Headline Indent
.ix Headline Font
.ix Headline Ruler
.ix Text Attributes
.ix Text Justification
.ix Text Indent
.ix Text Font
.ix Text Ruler
.ix Attributes
.ix Bold
.ix Italics
.ix Underscore Words
.ix Normal
The first panel has the five settings of the headline blocks, then the same
five of the text blocks.  A text field is on a row of dots, a choice is in
square brackets.
.table 20 27 10
Field | Values | Default
Headline Attributes | Normal, Bold, Italic, Underscore Words | Normal
Headline Justification | Left, Right, Center, Even | Left
Headline Indent | None, First, All but First, All | None
Headline Font | one of the 20 fonts | Font 4
Headline Ruler | tab stops, or empty | none
Text Attributes | Normal, Bold, Italic, Underscore Words | Normal
Text Justification | Left, Right, Center, Even | Left
Text Indent | None, First, All but First, All | None
Text Font | one of the 20 fonts | Font 2
Text Ruler | tab stops, or empty | none
.end
The default is what a panel shows for a document whose setting nobody has
changed.  A new document has a bold headline and evenly justified text with a
ruler at 5 ({n:format-default}), so its panel shows those.

{b:Attributes.}  The attribute of a format is the look that all the
characters of a block have unless the text itself says otherwise.  Bold,
Italic and Underscore Words are drawn on the screen and printed as bold,
italic and underlined text; the headline attribute applies to the whole
headline, its tag included.  An attribute of the text is added to the
attribute of the format ({n:format-order}).  The {k:F6} panel also offers
Bold Italics, and a block that has it keeps it; the panels then show the
choice as a fifth entry.  The attribute keys are in
{x:format-screen-attrkeys}.

The other fields are explained in {x:format-set-justification},
{x:format-set-indents}, {x:format-set-tabs} and {x:format-set-typefaces}.

.h3 Global Format (spacing) [format-fields-spacing]
.ix Line Spacing
.ix Headline Spacing
.ix Block Spacing
.ix Subsection Spacing
.ix Peer Section Spacing
.ix Indent per Level
.ix Auto Numbering
.ix Auto Numbering to Level
.ix Page Break Level
.ix Page Number Level
.ix First Block Type
.ix Print, Global Format field
.ix Ruler, Global Format field
.ix Winding Columns
.pic pictures/format-global-spacing.png 5 Global Format (spacing).
The second panel has fourteen fields: the spacings, which are numbers of
lines with two decimals, the indent of the sections on the screen, the
switch of the section tags and the depth to which they are given, and the
settings of the page, of the first block, of the ruler and of the columns.
.table 20 28 9
Field | Values | Default
Line Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | 1.00
Headline Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | 1.50
Block Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | 1.50
Subsection Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | 1.50
Peer Section Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | 2.00
Indent per Level | whole number | 0
Page Break Level | whole number | 0
Page Number Level | whole number | 0
Auto Numbering? | No, Yes | Yes
Auto Numbering to Level | whole number | 99
First Block Type | Headline, Joined | Headline
Print | Headlines and Text, Only Headlines, Only Text | Headlines and Text
Ruler | Relative, 10/inch, 12/inch, 15/inch | Relative
Winding Columns | whole number | 1
.end
Spacing is explained in {x:format-set-spacing}, Indent per Level in
{x:format-set-indents} and Auto Numbering in {x:format-set-tags}.

{b:Auto Numbering to Level} is the deepest level that has a section tag, on
the screen and in print: a section deeper than that has none.  The default
99 numbers every level; with 2 the headlines of levels 1 and 2 have a tag
and those of level 3 and deeper have not.  A level that has a Section Tag of
its own keeps it ({n:format-set-tags}).

{b:How deep sections nest.}  The format has no limit to the nesting of
sections that a person would meet: a document of 3000 nested levels is read,
shown and printed.  The levels 1 to 99 have a section tag, which is the number
of the place of the section (1.1.1.1 ...), and a section of level 100 or deeper
has none; its status line shows the number of its ancestor of level 99.  The
screen and the print give the same number to every section: the status line,
the tag before the headline, Go To, the cross references, the Section marker
and the printed headline.  Only the Global Format panel and the five Level
Format panels have settings of their own; a section deeper than level 5 takes
those of the Global Format panel.  The indent of a deeply nested section stops
24 cells short of the width of the text, on the screen and in print, so that
there is room left to read it, and a tag of more than 40 characters is broken
after a point when it is printed.  The deepest level of the table of contents
is a number from 1 to 99 (Contents Levels in {n:print-contents}).

{b:Page Break Level} starts a new page at every headline of that level or of
a higher one, in a print and in a preview.  The default 0 makes no page break
of its own.  With 1 each section of the first level, each chapter of a book,
starts a page; with 2 every section of the first and the second level does,
and so on.  The first headline of the document leaves no blank page, the
title page is still on a page of its own, and a part that is printed alone
begins on its first page.  A Draft print has the page breaks
too.  A Keep region is not split by a page break ({n:marker-keep}), and the
headline of a section that starts a page is at the top, without the space
that is added before a headline.  A level that is deeper than any section of
the document changes nothing.  To have a page break at some other place, use
a Page Break marker ({n:marker-page-break}).

{b:Page Number Level.}  The page number starts again at 1 at every headline of
this level or of a higher one (a smaller number); 0, the default, numbers the
pages of the whole document in one run.  With the level 1, every chapter
starts at page 1 and the pages that follow it, with the sections below it,
go on from there; with the level 2, every section of level 2 or 1 starts at
page 1.  The page on which the headline stands is the page 1, even when the
headline is in the middle of that page.  The Page Number marker of a header
or a footer, the table of contents and the cross-references that give a page
use these numbers.  Only the print has pages; the screen is unchanged.

{b:Print.}  Headlines and Text, the default, prints the whole document.  Only
Headlines prints the headline blocks only: the outline of the document, each
headline with its tag, under the one before with the headline spacing; the
table of contents is still made.  Only Text prints the text blocks only: no
headline, no tag and no table of contents.  The title page is printed in both.
The Editor shows the whole document whatever the field says; Only Text is
useful when the headlines are comments to the author.

{b:Ruler.}  The size of a ruler unit on the printed page.  Relative, the
default, makes a unit one eightieth of the width between the margins, so that
the ruler of the screen, which has 80 units, is as wide as the text; 10/inch,
12/inch and 15/inch make a unit a tenth, a twelfth or a fifteenth of an inch.
The unit is the one of the tab stops, of the widths of the columns and of the
gaps, of the left and right block indents (Block Indent) and of the indents of
the levels ({b:Indent per Level}, {b:Indent to Next Level}).  A tab stop, a
column or an indent that would be wider than the text is made as wide as the
text.  The screen is always 80 columns wide.  A block with a left indent of 20
starts 1.625 inches from the left edge of the text in Relative on a text 6.5
inches wide, and 2 inches from it at 10/inch.

{b:First Block Type.}  Headline, the default, prints the headline of a section
on a line of its own.  Joined prints the headline and the first block of text
of the section as one paragraph: the headline, in its own font and attributes,
at the start of the first line, then two spaces, then the text; the lines that
follow begin at the left margin.  The table of contents is the same.  The
Editor keeps the two blocks on two lines.  The Printer joins nothing, and puts
a line in the Error Log, when the headline and the text have different fonts
("Cannot join headline/text blocks - different fonts"), when Print is not
Headlines and Text ("Cannot join headline/text blocks - unprinted") or when a
block has several columns ("Cannot join multicolumn headline/text blocks"); the
two blocks are then printed apart.  The level panels have the same field for
the sections of one level.

{b:Winding Columns.}  The number of columns, 1 to 9, in which the printed text
is set; 1, the default, is no columns.  The text flows down one column and
winds to the top of the next when the column is full, and the words are
broken at the end of a line to the width of the column.  A headline keeps the
full width of the page, and so do the title page and the rows of a table.  The
Editor shows one column.  A block can have its own number, gutter and rule ({b:Winding Columns} of
the Named Block Format, which has the same layout panel,
{n:format-named-edit}) and a level its own.  With {k:F2} (or {k:Keypad +})
on the field the {b:Winding Column Layout} panel opens, with three fields:
Number of Columns, {b:Gutter Size} (the space between the columns, an inch
with up to three decimals, 0.250 by default) and {b:Vertical Rules} (None, the
default, Single, Double or Wide: a line between the columns, thin, thicker or
thickest; the Printer draws Double as one thicker line).  The Printer balances the last page of a text in columns.  {k:Ins} keeps the panel and {k:Esc} leaves it.
.pic pictures/format-global-winding.png 5 The Winding Column Layout panel, with two columns and a gutter of 0.3 inch.

.h3 Global Format (line numbering) [format-fields-linenum]
.ix Line numbering
.ix Numbered lines
.ix Lines, numbers in the margin
.ix Citing a line
.pic pictures/format-global-linenum.png 5 Global Format (line numbering).
The third panel numbers the printed lines of the document: each printed line
gets its number in the margin, in a small sans serif type, so that a number
is never taken for text.  The numbers are in a Print, in a Preview and in an
exported LaTeX file, all the same; they are not on the screen of the Editor.
A document that is not numbered is printed as before.
.table 18 30 9
Field | Values | Default
Numbering | Off, Pagewise, Running, Each Section | Off
Number Every | 1 to 99 | 1
Side | Left, Right, Outer, Inner | Left
Number Size | Tiny, Script, Footnote | Tiny
Number Font | Sans, Roman, Typewriter | Sans
Number Format | Arabic, Roman, Capital Roman | Arabic
Distance | 0.00 to 2.00 inches | 0.15
Equations | No, Yes | No
.end
{b:Numbering} says how the lines are counted.  Off prints no numbers.
Pagewise starts again at 1 at the top of each page, as pleadings and
contracts are numbered.  Running counts through the whole document, so that
a line is named by one number.  Each Section counts through the document
and starts again at 1 at each section of the first level; a document without
sections is counted as with Running.
{b:Number Every} puts a number on every n-th line only: with 5 the lines 5,
10, 15 and so on have a number and the others have none (the count is
the same).  {b:Side} puts the numbers at the left or the right of the text.
Outer and Inner are for pages that are printed on both sides: the odd
pages are the right-hand ones, so Outer puts the number at the right of an
odd page and at the left of an even one, and Inner the other way round.
{b:Number Size}, {b:Number Font} and {b:Number Format} are the look of the
number: its type size (smaller than the text), its type family and the
digits (1 2 3, i ii iii or I II III; a roman number shows 1 to 3999 and
the line 4000 and the lines after it are numbered in arabic digits).  {b:Distance} is the space between the
number and the text, in inches.  {b:Equations} Yes numbers the lines of a
displayed equation as lines of the text; with No a displayed equation takes
no number.
Number Every must be a whole number from 1 to 99, and Distance a number
from 0.00 to 2.00, or a message reports this and the panel is shown again.

.h3 Global Format (contents and lists) [format-fields-lists]
.ix Table of Contents Level
.ix Table of Figures
.ix Automatic list
.ix Marker counts
.pic pictures/format-global-lists.png 5 Global Format (contents and lists).
The fourth panel sets what the print makes of the contents, the index and the
lists of the document, and it is stored in the document.  It is the choice of
the document; the Print panel starts with it, and a change there is for one
print ({n:print-contents}, {n:print-index}).
.table 20 40
Field | What it does
Table of Contents Level | A number from 0 to 99: the deepest level of headline
 that the contents list.  0 means no contents.  A structured document that has no
 level set lists every level (99).  A document that is not structured, or has no
 headline, has no contents page.
Table of Figures | Automatic, On or Off for the list of the Figure markers.
Table of Pictures | The same for the names of the Picture markers.
Table of Tables | The same for the Table markers.
Index | The same for the index that the Index markers make.
Figure Markers, Picture Markers, Table Markers, Index Markers | The number of
 markers of the kind that the document has now.  It is shown, not a field.
.end
{b:Automatic} is the first choice of each list and the usual one: the list is
made when the document has at least one marker of its kind, and not otherwise.
The document keeps the count of each kind as you work: a marker that you type,
paste or bring back with Undo adds one, a marker that you delete, cut or take
away with Undo takes one off, and a block or a section that has markers does
the same for the markers inside it, so the list turns on with the first marker
and off with the last.  {b:On} makes the list whatever the count is (a list with
no entry is not printed) and {b:Off} never makes it; both stay in the document
until you change them.  The count is saved with the document.  When a count
cannot be right (it would go below 0, or it has reached 9999999) it is marked,
and the document is counted again the next time it is opened; the panel shows a
note of this beside the number.  A document that was made by an earlier
version, or by
another program, has no counts and is counted when it is opened.
Table of Contents Level must be a number from 0 to 99, or a message reports this and
the panel is shown again.

.h3 What counts as a line [format-linenum-lines]
.ix Line numbering, what is counted
.ix Numbered lines, tables
.ix Numbered lines, Keep blocks
Every printed line of a headline or of a block of text is a line, whether it
ends because the text wraps or because the text ends there.  A picture in a
block is one line, however high it is.  The space between blocks is not a
line and an empty block takes no number.  The lines of a Keep block are
numbered like the others.
Some printed lines have no number, and the count goes on past them:
{b:the rows of a table} (a block of several columns), {b:footnotes} and
{b:endnotes}, the headers and the footers, the Error Log page, and the lines
of a Keep block that floats to the top of a page ({n:marker-keep}).  When
a print fails, the banner and the marks of the errors are on the pages as
always, and the numbers do not move them.

.h3 Level 1 to Level 5 Format [format-fields-levels]
.ix Level 1 Format
.ix Level 2 Format
.ix Level 3 Format
.ix Level 4 Format
.ix Level 5 Format
.ix Section Tag, Level Format
.ix Inherited value, brackets
.ix Value in brackets
.pic pictures/format-level1.png 5 Level 1 Format (headline and text).
Each level has two panels, Level n Format (headline and text) and Level n
Format (spacing), with the title of the level in the title bar.  The first of
them has the same ten fields as the global panel; the second has the five
spacings, the Indent to Next Level, the First Block Type, the Section Tag and
the Winding Columns.
.table 24 20 13
Field | Values | Shows
Line Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | [1.00]
Headline Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | [1.50]
Block Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | [1.50]
Subsection Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | [1.50]
Peer Section Spacing | 0.00 to 99.99 | [2.00]
Indent to Next Level | whole number | [0]
First Block Type | Headline, Joined | [Headline]
Section Tag | text | []
Winding Columns | whole number | [1]
.end
Auto Numbering? and Auto Numbering to Level are set on the global panel only.
Indent to Next Level adds the indent of the sections below the level
({n:format-set-indents}); First Block Type joins the headline of the sections
of the level with their text; Winding Columns, with its gutter and rules, sets
the columns of the sections of the level ({n:format-fields-spacing}).

A level field shows, in brackets, the value that the level takes from the
global settings: [Bold], [Left], [1.50], [None] for a ruler that has no
stops.  A field that shows its bracket has no value of its own.  Pick another
choice, or type another value, and the level gets a value of its own; put the
bracket back (the first entry of the choice list, or an emptied text field)
and the level follows the global value again.  If the global value changes
later, the bracket changes with it.

.pic pictures/format-level1-spacing.png 5 Level 1 Format (spacing).

.h3 How the panels are applied [format-format-apply]
.ix Applying the Format panels
.ix Empty field in Format panels
When {k:Ins} is pressed the program first checks every number of every
panel, then applies each field whose value is not what it showed at the
start.  A field that was not touched is never changed, even when its
value is equal to the default.
.table 22 38
Case | Result
Global field set to its default | The global setting is the default again.
Global field emptied | The default is used.
Global ruler emptied | No tab stops.
Level field set to its bracket | The level follows the global value.
Level field emptied | The level follows the global value.
Level choice, first entry | The level follows the global value.
Any other value | The value is used.
Spacing typed with three decimals | The third decimal is dropped.
Whole number typed with a sign | Refused: whole numbers only.
.end
The checks are these: every spacing field must be a number with at most two
digits before the point and, if there is a point, digits after it (a
second point or a letter is refused, a leading point such as .5 is
accepted and means 0.50); every whole number field (Indent per Level, Page
Break Level, Page Number Level, Auto Numbering to Level, Winding Columns and
Indent to Next Level) must be made of digits only.  A
message names the field: "Line Spacing must be a number such as 1.50" or
"Indent per Level must be a whole number".  The panel that holds the faulty
field is shown again, and nothing has been applied; correct the field and
press {k:Ins} again.  A number is not checked against a range: 0 is allowed
for a spacing, and any whole number for the indent.
